## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 3-valued logic inside a constrained satisfaction task and to design a targeted backtracking propagation routine, mirroring real policy/rule engines.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics for ∧, ∨, ¬, and → via ¬A ∨ B
- Propagation of Unknowns to prune F-violating branches
- Backtracking with partial assignments in a small CSP

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch for Kleene-style SATish propagation
const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return order[a] < order[b] ? a : b; }
function or(a,b){ return order[a] > order[b] ? a : b; }
function imp(a,b){ return or(not(a), b); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does this scale with more variables and cyclic constraints?
- What pruning heuristics minimize backtracks in practice?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests design of tri-valued constraint systems with a custom operator and backtracking propagation, ensuring candidates reason about Kleene semantics and propagation rules.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic with T > U > F; ∧/∨ act as min/max.
- Custom operator Nand defined as not(and(x,y)).
- Backtracking propagation to prune domains and find a satisfying assignment.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Basic tri-valued lattice: T > U > F
const rank = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return rank[a] <= rank[b] ? a : b; }
function or(a,b){ return rank[a] >= rank[b] ? a : b; }
function nand(a,b){ return not(and(a,b)); }
function implies(p,q){ return or(not(p), q); }

// sample assignment
let F1='T', F2='F', F3='U', F4='F';
console.log(implies(or(F1,F2), F3)); // U
console.log(and(F2,F4)); // F
console.log(nand(F3,F4)); // T
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to N flags and arbitrary constraint sets?
- What changes when moving to a 4-valued logic or alternative lattices?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical logical deduction and simple SAT handling in a controlled, small-domain setting.

## Key Concepts
- Implication with negation
- Disjunction
- Satisfiability search
- Backtracking basics
- Early termination on first valid assignment

## Code Example
```javascript
function isSat(A,B) {
  // Clause1: (!A => B)  == (A || B)
  // Clause2: (B => A)   == (!B || A)
  // Clause3: (A || B)
  return (A || B) && (!B || A) && (A || B);
}
```

