## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply public announcements to epistemic models and design a practical solver that scales with model size.

## Key Concepts
- Kripke models and accessibility relations
- Public announcements and product update
- Knowledge operator Ki and post-update evaluation
- Linear-time propagation and fixpoint reasoning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal representation and product update sketch
function productUpdate(W, relations, V, pSet) {
  const Wp = W.filter(w => pSet.has(w));
  const Rp = {};
  for (const [AG, rel] of Object.entries(relations)) {
    Rp[AG] = new Map();
    for (const w of Wp) {
      Rp[AG].set(w, rel.get(w).filter(v => pSet.has(v)));
    }
  }
  return { W: Wp, relations: Rp };
}

function knows(pSet, w, agentRel) {
  const targets = agentRel.get(w) || [];
  return targets.every(u => pSet.has(u));
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How to extend to more agents or multi-modal knowledge (common knowledge)?
- How would you optimize for many sequential announcements or large models?
- What are potential numerical issues with non-deterministic updates in practice?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests extending Boolean logic to 3-valued systems with biconditionals in realistic feature-flag scenarios, plus constraint propagation and backtracking techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic for →, ∧, ∨ operations
- Biconditional as (A→B) ∧ (B→A)
- Constraint propagation with Unknown handling and backtracking
- Feature-flag rollout scenarios with partial information

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene values: F < U < T
const rank = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function implies(a,b){ const na = not(a); return [na, b].reduce((x,y)=>rank[x]>=rank[y]?x:y); }
function and(a,b){ return [a, b].reduce((x,y)=>rank[x]<=rank[y]?x:y); }
function or(a,b){ return [a, b].reduce((x,y)=>rank[x]>=rank[y]?x:y); }
```

## Approach
The propagation algorithm iteratively applies constraints, treating U as flexible until forced to T or F. When conflicts arise, it backtracks systematically through the remaining U values.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft and reason about 3-valued constraint systems and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued logic Kleene semantics
- Constraint propagation and backtracking
- Horn-like implication patterns

## Code Example
```javascript
function kleeneImply(a,b) {
  const notA = a==='T'?'F':a==='F'?'T':'U';
  // simplified Kleene-like rule: true if notA is F or b is T; unknown if either is U
  if (notA==='F' || b==='T') return 'T';
  if (notA==='U' || b==='U') return 'U';
  return 'F';
}
```

